{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06982","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06982","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06982","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masih","familyName":"Aminbeidokhti","name":"Aminbeidokhti, Masih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heitor Rapela","familyName":"Medeiros","name":"Medeiros, Heitor Rapela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Granger","name":"Granger, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pedersoli","name":"Pedersoli, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust and Efficient Finetuning of Vision Foundation Models via Implicit Ensembling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:07:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-19T15:33:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T02:34:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robust finetuning of foundation models seeks to improve out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization during in-distribution (ID) adaptation. We revisit Mixout, a stochastic regularizer that intermittently replaces finetuned parameters with a reference anchor, and study its effectiveness for robust finetuning of vision foundation models. We reinterpret Mixout as a single-run, weight-sharing implicit ensemble and analyze its expected OOD error through a bias-variance-covariance-locality (BVCL) lens. This perspective reveals three key factors that govern the ID-OOD trade-off: the choice of masking anchor, the mask resampling frequency, and mask sparsity. Guided by this analysis, we propose GMixout, which generalizes Mixout in three ways. First, it explicitly controls the masking period through a resampling-frequency hyperparameter. Second, it replaces the fixed pretrained anchor with an exponential moving average snapshot that adapts throughout training. Third, it uses sparse kernels to update only a small subset of parameters at each iteration, introducing no inference-time overhead and enabling large-model finetuning on consumer-grade GPUs. Experiments across five vision benchmarks show that GMixout consistently outperforms Mixout, Model Soups, and strong parameter-efficient finetuning baselines under both covariate shift and class imbalance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 9 figures, Neurips 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06982","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-20T05:15:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06470","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06470","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06470","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdülbaki","familyName":"Şanlan","name":"Şanlan, Abdülbaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatih","familyName":"Erol","name":"Erol, Fatih","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Murad","familyName":"Abu-Khalaf","name":"Abu-Khalaf, Murad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emre","familyName":"Koyuncu","name":"Koyuncu, Emre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Terrain-Aided Navigation Without Raycasting: A Sliding Grid Approach for Point Cloud Processing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T21:13:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:09:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-13T13:42:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-14T03:52:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the use of a point cloud measurement in terrain-aided navigation. Our goal is to aid an inertial navigation system, by exploring ways to generate a useful measurement innovation error for effective nonlinear state estimation. We compare two such measurement models that involve the scanning of a digital terrain elevation model: a) one that is based on typical ray-casting from a given pose, that returns the predicted point cloud measurement from that pose, and b) another computationally less intensive one that does not require raycasting and we refer to herein as a sliding grid. Besides requiring a pose, it requires the pattern of the point cloud measurement itself and returns a predicted point cloud measurement. We show that the sliding grid achieves comparable accuracy to raycasting, especially at higher altitudes, while being orders of magnitude faster computationally. By eliminating the need for raycasting, the sliding grid removes the dependency on dedicated GPU hardware or high-performance CPUs, making it well-suited for real-time terrain-aided navigation on resource-constrained embedded platforms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06470","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:47:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:47:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-14T08:20:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donghwan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Donghwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho","familyName":"Baek","name":"Baek, Jinho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timothy","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, Timothy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younghoon","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Younghoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kwangsik","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Kwangsik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongryool","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Jongryool","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongse","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jongse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kiwan","familyName":"Maeng","name":"Maeng, Kiwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cocoon: A System Architecture for Differentially Private Training with Correlated Noises"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:56:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-01T18:13:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:04:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine learning (ML) models memorize and leak training data, causing serious privacy issues to data owners. Training algorithms with differential privacy (DP) have been gaining attention as a solution. However, these algorithms add noise at each training iteration and degrade accuracy, limiting their real-world adoption. To improve accuracy, a new family of approaches adds carefully designed correlated noises, so that noises cancel out each other across iterations. We performed an extensive characterization study of these new mechanisms and show they incur non-negligible overheads when the model is relatively large or uses large embedding tables compared to the hardware capacity. Motivated by the analysis, we propose Cocoon, a framework for efficient training with correlated noises. Cocoon stores and processes the large noise history across CPU, GPU, and memory extension module, introduces optimizations for sparse embedding tables, and leverages to-be-commercialized near-memory processing (NMP) devices. On a real system with an FPGA-based NMP device prototype, Cocoon improves the performance by 1.23-10.82x."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in the Proceedings of 20th USENIX Symposium on Operating Systems Design and Implementation (OSDI'26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:43:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07215","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07215","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07215","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maojie","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Maojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Argyro","familyName":"Sasli","name":"Sasli, Argyro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexandra","familyName":"Junell","name":"Junell, Alexandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felipe Fontinele","familyName":"Nunes","name":"Nunes, Felipe Fontinele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Jing","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yu-Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christoffer","familyName":"Fremling","name":"Fremling, Christoffer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sam","familyName":"Rose","name":"Rose, Sam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theophile Jegou Du","familyName":"Laz","name":"Laz, Theophile Jegou Du","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benny","familyName":"Border","name":"Border, Benny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine Le","familyName":"Calloch","name":"Calloch, Antoine Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sushant Sharma","familyName":"Chaudhary","name":"Chaudhary, Sushant Sharma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailey","familyName":"Markoff","name":"Markoff, Hailey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avyukt","familyName":"Raghuvanshi","name":"Raghuvanshi, Avyukt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nabeel","familyName":"Rehemtulla","name":"Rehemtulla, Nabeel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesper","familyName":"Sollerman","name":"Sollerman, Jesper","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yashvi","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Yashvi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niharika","familyName":"Sravan","name":"Sravan, Niharika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Judy","familyName":"Adler","name":"Adler, Judy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tracy X.","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tracy X.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Dekany","name":"Dekany, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reed","familyName":"Riddle","name":"Riddle, Reed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mansi M.","familyName":"Kasliwal","name":"Kasliwal, Mansi M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew J.","familyName":"Graham","name":"Graham, Matthew J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael W.","familyName":"Coughlin","name":"Coughlin, Michael W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AppleCiDEr. II. SpectraNet: A Spectroscopic Neural Network Classifier for Transients Demonstrated on ZTF Follow-up Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:49:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T14:20:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:02:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T00:19:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-10T01:10:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-20T20:24:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:27:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1538-3873/ae998a","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time-domain surveys such as the Zwicky Transient Facility have opened a new frontier in the discovery and characterization of transients. While photometric light curves provide broad temporal coverage, spectroscopic observations remain crucial for physical interpretation and source classification. However, existing spectral analysis methods, often reliant on template fitting or parametric models, are limited in their ability to capture the complex and evolving spectra characteristic of such sources, which are sometimes only available at low resolution. In this work, we introduce SpectraNet, a deep convolutional neural network designed to learn robust representations of optical spectra from transients. Our model combines multi-scale convolution kernels and pooling operations to extract features from preprocessed spectra in a hierarchical and interpretable manner. We train and validate SpectraNet on low-resolution time-series spectra obtained from the Spectral Energy Distribution Machine and other instruments, demonstrating better performance in classification compared to other known pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages,9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07215","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:36Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:43:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06348","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06348","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06348","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Quigg","name":"Quigg, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Perspectives and Questions: Toward an Expansive Agenda for Particle Physics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T18:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:03:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-11T02:02:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-14T00:17:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Global celebration greeted the 2012 discovery at CERN's Large Hadron Collider of a particle that matches the textbook description of the Higgs boson. That achievement validated a remarkable chain of theoretical reasoning that combined the prescriptive notion of electroweak gauge symmetry with a simple, but \\emph{ad hoc}, embodiment of spontaneous symmetry breaking. It was made possible by generational triumphs of accelerator art and experimental technique, and by human resourcefulness and collaboration on a global scale, all sustained by the enlightened support of many governments and institutions.\n Some imagine that, once the keystone of the standard model of particle physics has been set, our subject is over. Others worry that we may be at an impasse because no comparable wonders have appeared, leaving us without well-defined clues to a more complete paradigm. I am neither so readily satisfied nor so {easily} discouraged: we have so much more to learn! This essay surveys many questions that, taken together, constitute an inspiring array of opportunities to enhance our understanding of the physical world."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages, 1 figure. One typo corrected, one reference added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06348","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:44:13Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:44:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-14T02:17:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06764","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06764","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06764","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuyen","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tuyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mária","familyName":"Kieferová","name":"Kieferová, Mária","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Theoretical Guarantees of Variational Quantum Algorithm with Guiding States"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:45:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:33:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-07T20:25:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-09T02:03:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Machine learning provides a powerful framework for predicting ground-state properties across families of quantum many-body problems, enabling amortized inference and reducing the cost of repeated simulations. Variational quantum algorithms (VQAs) are promising candidates for implementing such learnable solvers on quantum computers, yet rigorous guarantees for convergence and generalization remain scarce. Motivated by the fact that standard quantum phase estimation (QPE) provides provable performance when given a guiding state with non-trivial overlap with the ground state, we introduce a variational quantum algorithm with guiding states aiming towards predicting ground-state properties of quantum many-body systems. We then develop a proof technique-the linearization trick-that maps the training dynamics of the algorithm to those of a kernel model. This connection yields theoretical guarantees on both convergence and generalization for the VQA under the guiding state assumption. Our analysis shows that guiding states facilitate convergence, suppress finite-size error terms, and ensure stability across system dimensions. Finally, we validate our findings with numerical experiments on 2D random Heisenberg models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06764","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-09T05:54:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ajinkya","familyName":"Gaikwad","name":"Gaikwad, Ajinkya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Parameterized Complexity of $s$-Club Cluster Edge Deletion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:30:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:51:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T07:29:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:29:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T06:14:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:27:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-03T15:29:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:43:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-30T12:15:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-09-01T01:04:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the parameterized and kernelization complexity of the \\emph{\\textsc{$s$-Club Cluster Edge Deletion}} problem, a distance-bounded generalization of \\emph{\\textsc{Cluster Edge Deletion}}. Given a graph $G=(V,E)$ and integers $k,s$, the goal is to delete at most $k$ edges so that every resulting connected component has diameter at most $s$.\n On the structural side, we settle an open question of Montecchiani, Ortali, Piselli, and Tappini (\\emph{Theoretical Computer Science}, 2023) by proving W[1]-hardness parameterized by pathwidth plus the maximum number of allowed $s$-clubs, and consequently by treewidth plus this parameter. Thus, the diameter bound $s$ is inecessary for tractability under these parameters. In contrast, we show that dependence on \\(s\\) is unnecessary for several structural parameters: the problem is fixed-parameter tractable when parameterized by treedepth, neighborhood diversity, or cluster vertex deletion number, generalizing known results for $s=1.$\n We further prove that no polynomial kernel exists when parameterized by vertex cover, even for $s=2$. On the positive side, we present an FPT bicriteria approximation scheme for graphs excluding long induced cycles, running in time $f(k,1/ε)\\cdot n^{\\mathcal{O}(1)}$ and producing a solution of size at most $k$ whose components have diameter at most $(1+ε)s$.\n Finally, we initiate the study of the directed variant, \\textsc{$s$-Club Cluster Arc Deletion}, and prove that it is W[1]-hard parameterized by $k$, even on directed acyclic graphs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:01:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:01:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-01T05:01:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06612","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06612","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06612","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahua","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiahua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Bridge from Audio to Video: Phoneme-Viseme Alignment Allows Every Face to Speak Multiple Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T03:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:21:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-06T15:51:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-07T00:56:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech-driven talking face synthesis (TFS) focuses on generating lifelike facial animations from speech input. Current TFS models perform well in English but struggle with non-English languages, producing inaccurate mouth shapes and rigid facial expressions. These limitations are mainly caused by English-dominated training datasets and the lack of cross-language generalization ability.To address these challenges, we propose Multilingual Experts (MuEx), a novel framework featuring a Phoneme-Guided Mixture-of-Experts (PG-MoE) architecture that employs phonemes and visemes as universal intermediaries to bridge the gap between audio and visual modalities, enabling lifelike multilingual TFS. We extract speech and visual features as phonemes and visemes, respectively, which represent the basic units of speech sounds and mouth movements, to alleviate linguistic differences and dataset bias.Furthermore, we introduce the Phoneme-Viseme Alignment Mechanism (PV-Align), which establishes robust cross-modal correspondences between phonemes and visemes to improve audiovisual synchronization. In addition, we construct a Multilingual Talking Face Dataset (MTFD) comprising 12 diverse languages with 95.04 hours of high-quality videos for training and evaluating multilingual TFS performance.Extensive experiments demonstrate that MuEx achieves superior performance across all languages in MTFD and exhibits effective zero-shot generalization to unseen languages without additional training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06612","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-07T02:15:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06628","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06628","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06628","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Babak","familyName":"Vakili","name":"Vakili, Babak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Classical Polymerization of the Bianchi I Model with Deformed Poisson Structure"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:20:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-24T00:10:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1142/s0219887826500349","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the dynamics of the Bianchi~I cosmological model in the presence of both polymer quantization effects and an exponential deformation of the Poisson algebra. Starting from the Hamiltonian formulation, we derive the polymer-deformed equations of motion and analyze their solutions for the contracting branch of the model. In contrast with the undeformed classical dynamics, the exponential deformation with suitable values of deformation parameters, produces a noticeably slower evolution of the volume variable and leads to a stabilization of the anisotropy parameters, which remain bounded throughout the evolution. No removal of the initial singularity is observed; however, the deformation significantly modifies the asymptotic behavior, offering a mechanism to suppress anisotropic shear near the singularity. Our results are illustrated through analytic solutions, highlighting the qualitative differences between the standard and the polymer--deformed Bianchi~I cosmology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 4 figures, to appear in IJGMMP, This is the first part of a two-part article, the second part of which, titled \"Chaos and Epoch Structure in the Deformed Mixmaster Universe\" is being prepared for upload to the arXiv"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06628","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:50:43Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:50:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-24T02:11:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rihab","familyName":"Gharbi","name":"Gharbi, Rihab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wissem","familyName":"Jedidi","name":"Jedidi, Wissem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salah","familyName":"Khardani","name":"Khardani, Salah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Frédéric","familyName":"Ouimet","name":"Ouimet, Frédéric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Bernstein polynomial approach for the estimation of cumulative distribution functions in the presence of missing data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"62G05, 62E20, 62G08, 62G20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:03:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-27T14:56:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-07T01:59:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/s00362-026-01851-w","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study nonparametric estimation of univariate cumulative distribution functions (CDFs) pertaining to data missing at random. The proposed estimators smooth the inverse probability weighted (IPW) empirical CDF with the Bernstein operator, yielding monotone, $[0,1]$-valued curves that automatically adapt to bounded supports. We analyze two versions: a pseudo estimator that uses known propensities and a feasible estimator that uses propensities estimated nonparametrically from discrete auxiliary variables, the latter scenario being much more common in practice. For both, we derive pointwise bias and variance expansions, establish the optimal polynomial degree $m$ with respect to the mean integrated squared error, and prove the asymptotic normality. A key finding is that the feasible estimator has a smaller variance than the pseudo estimator by an explicit nonnegative correction term. We also develop an efficient degree selection procedure via least-squares cross-validation. Monte Carlo experiments show that, for small to moderate sample sizes, the Bernstein-smoothed pseudo and feasible estimators outperform their unsmoothed counterparts and the integrated version of the IPW kernel density estimator proposed by Dubnicka (2009), under certain models. A real-data application to fasting plasma glucose from the 2017-2018 NHANES survey illustrates the method in a practical setting. All code needed to reproduce our analyses is readily accessible on GitHub."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 2 figures, 10 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:05Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-07T04:58:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06928","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06928","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06928","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiangning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhuang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Lizhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"IAR2: Improving Autoregressive Visual Generation with Semantic-Detail Associated Token Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:08:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:43:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-27T05:34:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-28T00:36:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive models have emerged as a powerful paradigm for visual content creation, but often overlook the intrinsic structural properties of visual data. Our prior work, IAR, initiated a direction to address this by reorganizing the visual codebook based on embedding similarity, thereby improving generation robustness. However, it is constrained by the rigidity of pre-trained codebooks and the inaccuracies of hard, uniform clustering. To overcome these limitations, we propose IAR2, an advanced autoregressive framework that enables a hierarchical semantic-detail synthesis process. At the core of IAR2 is a novel Semantic-Detail Associated Dual Codebook, which decouples image representations into a semantic codebook for global semantic information and a detail codebook for fine-grained refinements. It expands the quantization capacity from a linear to a polynomial scale, significantly enhancing expressiveness. To accommodate this dual representation, we propose a Semantic-Detail Autoregressive Prediction scheme coupled with a Local-Context Enhanced Autoregressive Head, which performs hierarchical prediction-first the semantic token, then the detail token-while leveraging a local context window to enhance spatial coherence. Furthermore, for conditional generation, we introduce a Progressive Attention-Guided Adaptive CFG mechanism that dynamically modulates the guidance scale for each token based on its relevance to the condition and its temporal position in the generation sequence, improving conditional alignment without sacrificing realism. Extensive experiments demonstrate that IAR2 sets a new state-of-the-art for autoregressive image generation, achieving a FID of 1.50 on ImageNet. Our model not only surpasses previous methods in performance but also demonstrates superior computational efficiency, highlighting the effectiveness of our structured, coarse-to-fine generation strategy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06928","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:44Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-28T02:53:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06998","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06998","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06998","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Wilhelm","name":"Wilhelm, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franz","familyName":"Freitag","name":"Freitag, Franz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Tzschoppe","name":"Tzschoppe, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thilo","familyName":"Pionteck","name":"Pionteck, Thilo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Rapid Makespan Predictions for Heterogeneous Systems with Programmable Logic"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:20:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-15T00:57:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/norcas66540.2025.11231293","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Heterogeneous computing systems, which combine general-purpose processors with specialized accelerators, are increasingly important for optimizing the performance of modern applications. A central challenge is to decide which parts of an application should be executed on which accelerator or, more generally, how to map the tasks of an application to available devices. Predicting the impact of a change in a task mapping on the overall makespan is non-trivial. While there are very capable simulators, these generally require a full implementation of the tasks in question, which is particularly time-intensive for programmable logic. A promising alternative is to use a purely analytical function, which allows for very fast predictions, but abstracts significantly from reality. Bridging the gap between theory and practice poses a significant challenge to algorithm developers. This paper aims to aid in the development of rapid makespan prediction algorithms by providing a highly flexible evaluation framework for heterogeneous systems consisting of CPUs, GPUs and FPGAs, which is capable of collecting real-world makespan results based on abstract task graph descriptions. We analyze to what extent actual makespans can be predicted by existing analytical approaches. Furthermore, we present common challenges that arise from high-level characteristics such as data transfer overhead and device congestion in heterogeneous systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To be published on NorCAS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06998","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-15T02:24:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandra","familyName":"Mantovani","name":"Mantovani, Sandra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariano","familyName":"Messora","name":"Messora, Mariano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pretorsion theories in prenormal categories"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Category Theory (math.CT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"18E40 (Primary) 18A40, 18A32 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:05:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-09T14:12:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-10T00:56:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.jpaa.2026.108239","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper we extend several classical results on pointed torsion theories -- also known as torsion pairs -- to the setting of non-pointed torsion theories defined via kernels and cokernels relative to a fixed class of trivial objects (often referred to as pretorsion theories). Our results are developed in the recently introduced framework of (non-pointed) prenormal categories and other related contexts. Within these settings, we recover some characterisations of torsion and torsion-free subcategories, as well as the classical correspondences between torsion theories and closure operators. We also suitably extend a correspondence between torsion theories and (stable) factorisation systems on the ambient category, known in the homological case. Some of these results are then further specialised to an appropriate notion of hereditary torsion theory. Finally, we apply the developed theory to construct new examples of pretorsion theories."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages. Revised and accepted for publication in the Journal of Pure and Applied Algebra"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-10T02:24:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sercan","familyName":"Kaya","name":"Kaya, Sercan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bayram","familyName":"Tekin","name":"Tekin, Bayram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can Fractional Time Operators Reproduce Gravitational-Wave Memory? A No-Go Result"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:01:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-31T13:20:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:54:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We initiate an investigation into whether fractional calculus, with its intrinsic long-tailed memory and nonlocal features, can provide a viable model for gravitational-wave memory effects. We consider two toy constructions: ($i$) a fractional modification of the linearized Einstein field equations using a sequential Caputo operator; and ($ii$) a fractionalized quadrupole formula in which the same operator acts on the source moment. Both constructions yield history-dependent responses with small memory-like offsets. However, in all cases we studied, the signal decays to zero at late times, failing to reproduce the permanent displacement predicted by General Relativity. We showed that, under asymptotic and spatial flatness of spacetime, the solutions of the proposed models decay to zero at late times when the time derivatives of the perturbed metric are temporally localized and bounded at each spatial point. Therefore, our results constitute a no-go demonstration: naive fractionalization is insufficient to model the permanent offset in the metric without explicitly building in flux-balance laws or asymptotic symmetry structure. We argue that any successful model must incorporate fractional kernels directly into the hereditary flux-balance integral of General Relativity while preserving gauge invariance and dimensional consistency. We also discuss possible connections to modified gravity and the absence of memory in spacetime with $D\u0026gt;4$ dimensions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 6 figures, significantly extended; version to appear in the proceedings of 60 Years in Mathematical Physics: Gurses-Fest issue of Journal of Physics: Conference Series (JPCS, IOP Publishing)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:00Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:31:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoting","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuhan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Qiuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhennan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhennan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijie","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhongwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiuyang","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyong","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Zheyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GPU-MetaD: Full-Life-Cycle GPU Accelerated Metadynamics with Machine Learning Potentials"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:42:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-23T12:26:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-24T01:52:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale molecular dynamics simulations with high accuracy have been increasingly popular for their capability to bridge the gap between atomistic modeling and mesoscale phenomena. Both machine learning potentials and enhanced sampling approaches offer substantial improvements in high-accuracy simulation efficiency, which can be further boosted through GPU acceleration. However, an efficient framework combining these advances for extending simulations to large systems and long timescales remains elusive. In this work, we proposed a full-life-cycle GPU accelerated metadynamics simulations package GPU-MetaD. Benchmarking across molecular, interface, and bulk systems demonstrates that GPU-MetaD efficiently handles diverse atomic systems and delivers an order-of-magnitude performance improvement. Building on this demonstrated capability, it enables ab-initio-level rare-event sampling for systems comprising millions of atoms on a typical single GPU. This capability allows us to reveal a previously unknown size-dependent two-step nucleation mechanism in gallium nitride (GaN), highlighting the potential of GPU-MetaD for uncovering complex rare events in realistic large-scale materials systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:27Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-24T04:17:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06835","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06835","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06835","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongjian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyun","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Changyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resilient Multi-Dimensional Consensus and Distributed Optimization against Agent-Based and Denial-of-Service Attacks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:56:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T10:32:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:46:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T06:05:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:12:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we consider the resilient multi-dimensional consensus and distributed optimization problems of multi-agent systems (MASs) in the presence of both agent-based and denial-of-service (DoS) attacks. The considered agent-based attacks can cover malicious, Byzantine, and stubborn agents. The links between agents in the network can be blocked by DoS attacks, which may lead the digraph to be time-varying and even disconnected. The objective is to ensure that the remaining benign agents achieve consensus. To this end, an \"auxiliary point\"-based resilient control algorithm is proposed for MASs. Under the proposed algorithm, each healthy agent constructs a \"safe kernel\" utilizing the states of its in-neighbors and updates its state toward a specific point within this kernel at each iteration. If an agent cannot receive its neighbors' states owing to DoS attacks, it will use the states received immediately before the DoS period. Moreover, a resilient multi-dimensional distributed optimization (RMDO) algorithm is also proposed. Theoretical proofs and numerical examples are presented to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed algorithms."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06835","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:33Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-19T02:54:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07252","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07252","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07252","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arshad","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Arshad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mudassar","familyName":"Sabir","name":"Sabir, Mudassar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gauge dependence of scalar-induced gravitational waves from isocurvature perturbations: Analytical results"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:17:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:47:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:27:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:17:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T14:02:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-17T02:20:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/tby4-thbh","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analytically study the gauge dependence of scalar-induced gravitational waves (SIGWs) sourced by primordial isocurvature perturbations during radiation domination (RD), working across nine gauges. Through analytical integrations of the kernels supported by graphical comparison we identify a clear dichotomy. We find that in some gauges viz. the uniform-density (UD), total-matter (TM), uniform-curvature (UC), comoving-orthogonal (CO) and transverse-traceless (TT) gauges the energy density grows polynomially in conformal time $η^n$, where $n$ varies from $2$ to $8$. While in rest of the gauges viz. the longitudinal (Long.), uniform-expansion (UE), Newtonian-motion (Nm), and N-body (Nb) gauges the late-time energy spectrum converges, and SIGWs behave as radiation. For subhorizon modes ($ kη\\gg 1 $), the divergence becomes severe, showing that SIGWs are gauge-dependent observables in this regime. We resolve it through a kernel projection that isolates the luminal, freely propagating gravitational wave components (oscillating as $\\sin(kη)$ and $\\cos(kη)$), eliminating spurious contributions. The resulting kernel decays as $ (kη)^{-1} $ and yields a finite, gauge-independent late-time spectrum, confirming that only luminal modes represent physical SIGWs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"36 pages, 27 figures, published in Phys. Rev. D"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07252","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:05:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:05:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-17T04:56:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07224","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07224","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07224","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yongjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoshiteru","familyName":"Hidaka","name":"Hidaka, Yoshiteru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Smaluk","name":"Smaluk, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Timur","familyName":"Shaftan","name":"Shaftan, Timur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sextupole reduction via chaos suppression at the National Synchrotron Light Source II"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:57:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:00:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-11T20:13:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-09T01:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/1z4n-m4gl","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We revisit the nonlinear lattice design approach for the National Synchrotron Light Source II (NSLS-II) storage ring. By suppressing chaos, we identify alternative sextupole configurations to the original design, which relied on the conventional strategy of simultaneously minimizing Resonance Driving Terms (RDT) and Amplitude-Dependent Detuning (ADD). These alternatives achieve comparable performance while requiring fewer sextupoles. A detailed comparison of two representative solutions is presented and supported by experimental validation. Our results show that the dynamic aperture correlates more strongly with global chaos than with individual RDTs, and that the importance of minimizing ADD may have been overstated in earlier design strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 12 figures, accepted by the journal Physical Review Accelerators and Beams on Feb. 11, 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07224","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-09T02:09:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06738","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06738","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06738","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyi","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Boyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinbing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouhan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhouhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AWM: Accurate Weight-Matrix Fingerprint for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:51:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-03T09:39:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:47:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-14T13:03:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:32:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Protecting the intellectual property of large language models (LLMs) is crucial, given the substantial resources required for their training. Consequently, there is an urgent need for both model owners and third parties to determine whether a suspect LLM is trained from scratch or derived from an existing base model. However, the intensive post-training processes that models typically undergo-such as supervised fine-tuning, extensive continued pretraining, reinforcement learning, multi-modal extension, pruning, and upcycling-pose significant challenges to reliable identification. In this work, we propose a training-free fingerprinting method based on weight matrices. We leverage the Linear Assignment Problem (LAP) and an unbiased Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) similarity to neutralize the effects of parameter manipulations, yielding a highly robust and high-fidelity similarity metric. On a comprehensive testbed of 60 positive and 90 negative model pairs, our method demonstrates exceptional robustness against all six aforementioned post-training categories while exhibiting a near-zero risk of false positives. By achieving perfect scores on all classification metrics, our approach establishes a strong basis for reliable model lineage verification. Moreover, the entire computation completes within 30s on an NVIDIA 3090 GPU. The code is available at https://github.com/LUMIA-Group/AWM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06738","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:18Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:46:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingxi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhen","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Lizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhi","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Hongzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Relational Database Distillation: From Structured Tables to Condensed Graph Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Databases (cs.DB)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:05:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:46:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-20T13:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T03:19:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Relational databases (RDBs) underpin the majority of global data management systems, where information is structured into multiple interdependent tables. To effectively use the knowledge within RDBs for predictive tasks, recent advances leverage graph representation learning to capture complex inter-table relations as multi-hop dependencies. Despite achieving state-of-the-art performance, these methods remain hindered by the prohibitive storage overhead and excessive training time, due to the massive scale of the database and the computational burden of intensive message passing across interconnected tables. To alleviate these concerns, we propose and study the problem of Relational Database Distillation (RDD). Specifically, we aim to distill large-scale RDBs into compact heterogeneous graphs while retaining the predictive power (i.e., utility) required for training graph-based models. Multi-modal column information is preserved through node features, and primary-foreign key relations are encoded via heterogeneous edges, thereby maintaining both data fidelity and relational structure. To ensure adaptability across diverse downstream tasks without engaging the traditional, inefficient bi-level distillation framework, we further design a kernel ridge regression-guided objective with pseudo-labels, which produces quality features for the distilled graph. Extensive experiments on multiple real-world RDBs demonstrate that our solution substantially reduces the data size while maintaining competitive performance on classification and regression tasks, creating an effective pathway for scalable learning with RDBs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:59Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Martin","name":"Martin, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Apurva","familyName":"Patil","name":"Patil, Apurva","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongmei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dongmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Model Predictive Path Integral Control for Roll-to-Roll Manufacturing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:54:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:00:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.ifacol.2025.12.260.","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Roll-to-roll (R2R) manufacturing is a continuous processing technology essential for scalable production of thin-film materials and printed electronics, but precise control remains challenging due to subsystem interactions, nonlinearities, and process disturbances. This paper proposes a Model Predictive Path Integral (MPPI) control formulation for R2R systems, leveraging a GPU-based Monte-Carlo sampling approach to efficiently approximate optimal controls online. Crucially, MPPI easily handles non-differentiable cost functions, enabling the incorporation of complex performance criteria relevant to advanced manufacturing processes. A case study is presented that demonstrates that MPPI significantly improves tension regulation performance compared to conventional model predictive control (MPC), highlighting its suitability for real-time control in advanced manufacturing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:49Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07318","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07318","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunhao","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Yunhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghao","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Qinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuehan","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xuehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lai","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Lai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Artificial Hippocampus Networks for Efficient Long-Context Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:59:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:04:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T07:08:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:25:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long-sequence modeling faces a fundamental trade-off between the efficiency of compressive fixed-size memory in RNN-like models and the fidelity of lossless growing memory in attention-based Transformers. Inspired by the Multi-Store Model in cognitive science, we introduce a memory framework of artificial neural networks. Our method maintains a sliding window of the Transformer's KV cache as lossless short-term memory, while a learnable module termed Artificial Hippocampus Network (AHN) recurrently compresses out-of-window information into a fixed-size compact long-term memory. To validate this framework, we instantiate AHNs using modern RNN-like architectures, including Mamba2, DeltaNet, and GatedDeltaNet to augment open-weight LLMs. We also propose an efficient self-distillation training method where the base model's all parameters are frozen and only the parameters from AHNs are optimized. For inference, our method sets a default large sliding window size of 32k for attention, and AHNs activate only when the sequence length exceeds the 32k window, addressing the quadratic-complexity issue of attention that emerges at that scale. Extensive experiments on long-context benchmarks LV-Eval and InfiniteBench demonstrate that AHN-augmented models consistently outperform sliding window baselines and achieve performance comparable or even superior to full-attention models, while substantially reducing computational and memory requirements. For instance, augmenting the Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct with AHNs reduces inference FLOPs by 40.5% and memory cache by 74.0%, while improving its average score on LV-Eval (128k sequence length) from 4.41 to 5.88. Code is available at: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code: https://github.com/ByteDance-Seed/AHN"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07318","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:07:04Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-18T03:07:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06249","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06249","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06249","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toshiki","familyName":"Nakai","name":"Nakai, Toshiki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ravi Kiran","familyName":"Chikkala","name":"Chikkala, Ravi Kiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lena Sophie","familyName":"Oberkircher","name":"Oberkircher, Lena Sophie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Jennings","name":"Jennings, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natalia","familyName":"Skachkova","name":"Skachkova, Natalia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tatiana","familyName":"Anikina","name":"Anikina, Tatiana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesujoba Oluwadara","familyName":"Alabi","name":"Alabi, Jesujoba Oluwadara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TRepLiNa: Layer-wise CKA+REPINA Alignment Improves Low-Resource Machine Translation in Aya-23 8B"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T17:36:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-11T15:03:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:39:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-28T19:06:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:04:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T16:04:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:40:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-10T18:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-12-11T01:54:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The 2025 Multimodal Models for Low-Resource Contexts and Social Impact (MMLoSo) Language Challenge addresses one of India's most pressing linguistic gaps: the lack of resources for its diverse low-resource languages (LRLs). In this study, we investigate whether enforcing cross-lingual similarity in specific internal layers of a decoder-only multilingual large language model (LLM) can improve translation quality from LRL to high-resource language (HRL). Specifically, we combine Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA), a similarity metric that encourages representations of different languages to align, with REPINA, a regularization method that constrains parameter updates to remain close to the pretrained model, into a joint method we call TRepLiNa. In this research project, we experiment with zero-shot, few-shot, and fine-tuning settings using Aya-23 8B with QLoRA across MMLoSo shared task language pairs (Mundari, Santali, Bhili) with Hindi/English pivots. Our results show that aligning mid-level layers using TRepLiNa (CKA+REPINA) is a low-cost, practical approach to improving LRL translation, especially in data-scarce settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"It is work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06249","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:41:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:41:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-11T03:05:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06813","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06813","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06813","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgio","familyName":"Di Russo","name":"Di Russo, Giorgio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Tokareva","name":"Tokareva, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scalar Quasinormal modes in Reissner--Nordström black holes: implications for Weak Gravity Conjecture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"General Relativity and Quantum Cosmology (gr-qc)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:41:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T09:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:26:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Microscopic charged black holes can provide possibilities to test the consistency of the effective field theory (EFT) corrections to Einstein-Maxwell theory. A particularly interesting result is fixing the sign of a certain combination of EFT couplings from the requirement that all charged black holes should be able to evaporate (Weak Gravity Conjecture). In our work, we analysed the EFT corrections to a set of zero-damping quasinormal modes (QNMs) of the scalar wave probe in a nearly extremal Reissner-Nordström black hole. We review the duality of this setup to the problem of the quantum Seiberg-Witten curve of $N=2$ Super-Yang-Mills theory with three flavors. We provide an analytic result for the EFT corrections to the QNMs obtained from the quantization condition imposed on the Seiberg-Witten cycle. Our main result is that the causality requirement of the gravitational theory formulated for the QNMs translates to the same condition on EFT couplings as the one appearing in the Weak Gravity Conjecture."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06813","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:02Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:58:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06786","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06786","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06786","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Aharonian","name":"Aharonian, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunxiang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yunxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Den