ivenName":"Fengrong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Fengrong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejun","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Kejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan-chuan","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yuan-chuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiong","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Xiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oscar","familyName":"Macias","name":"Macias, Oscar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Giant Peanut-shaped Ultra-High-Energy Gamma-Ray Emitter Off the Galactic Plane"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:11:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:35:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-26T02:43:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:48:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ultra-high-energy (UHE), exceeding 100 TeV (10^12 electronvolts), γ-rays manifests extreme particle acceleration in astrophysical sources. Recent observations by γ-ray telescopes, particularly by the Large High Altitude Air Shower Observatory (LHAASO), have revealed a few tens of UHE sources, indicating numerous Galactic sources capable of accelerating particles to PeV (10^15 electronvolts) energies. However, discerning the dominant acceleration mechanisms (leptonic versus hadronic), the relative contributions of specific source classes, and the role of particle transport in shaping their observed emission are central goals of modern UHE astrophysics. Here we report the discovery of a giant UHE γ-ray emitter at -17.5° off the Galactic plane - a region where UHE γ-ray sources are rarely found. The emitter exhibits a distinctive asymmetric shape, resembling a giant \"Peanut\" spanning 0.45° \\times 4.6°, indicative of anisotropic particle distribution over a large area. A highly aged millisecond pulsar (MSP) J0218+4232 is the sole candidate accelerator positionally coincident with the Peanut region. Its association with UHE γ-rays extending to 0.7 PeV, if confirmed, would provide the first evidence of a millisecond pulsar powering PeV particles. Such a finding challenges prevailing models, which posit that millisecond pulsars cannot sustain acceleration to PeV energies. The detection reveals fundamental gaps in understanding particle acceleration, cosmic-ray transport, and interstellar magnetic field effects, potentially revealing new PeV accelerator (PeVatron) classes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06786","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:25Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:03:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06303","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06303","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06303","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Qian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongbo","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Zhongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qipeng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Biqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SDAR: A Synergistic Diffusion-AutoRegression Paradigm for Scalable Sequence Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:29:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T02:55:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:23:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-18T20:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:38:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose SDAR, a Synergistic Diffusion-Autoregression paradigm that unifies the training efficiency of autoregressive models with the parallel inference capability of diffusion. Instead of costly end-to-end diffusion training, SDAR performs a lightweight paradigm conversion that transforms a well-trained autoregressive (AR) model into a blockwise diffusion model through brief, data-efficient adaptation. During inference, SDAR generates sequences autoregressively across blocks for global coherence while decoding all tokens within each block in parallel via a discrete diffusion process. Extensive experiments show that AR models remain substantially more compute-efficient than masked diffusion models, providing a strong foundation for adaptation. Building on this insight, SDAR achieves efficient AR-to-diffusion conversion with minimal cost, preserving AR-level performance while enabling parallel generation. Scaling studies across dense and Mixture-of-Experts architectures confirm that SDAR scales without compromise: larger models exhibit stronger robustness to block size and decoding thresholds, yielding greater speedups without accuracy loss. Beyond efficiency, SDAR demonstrates enhanced reasoning and domain adaptability. Our 30B MoE model surpasses its AR counterpart on challenging scientific reasoning benchmarks such as GPQA and ChemBench, and gains further improvements under test-time scaling methods like majority voting and pass@k. Together, these results establish SDAR as a practical paradigm that combines the strengths of autoregression and diffusion for scalable, high-throughput reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report. 40 pages, Inference speedup analysis added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06303","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:43:09Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:43:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-21T03:42:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07108","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07108","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07108","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Lingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rashed","familyName":"Shelim","name":"Shelim, Rashed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walid","familyName":"Saad","name":"Saad, Walid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naren","familyName":"Ramakrishnan","name":"Ramakrishnan, Naren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Theoretically-Grounded Codebook for Digital Semantic Communications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T15:01:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:53:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T20:45:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:08:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The use of a learnable codebook provides an efficient way for semantic communications to map vector-based high-dimensional semantic features onto discrete symbol representations required in digital communication systems. In this paper, the problem of codebook-enabled quantization mapping for digital semantic communications is studied from the perspective of information theory. Particularly, a novel theoretically-grounded codebook design is proposed for jointly optimizing quantization efficiency, transmission efficiency, and robust performance. First, a formal equivalence is established between the one-to-many synonymous mapping defined in semantic information theory and the many-to-one quantization mapping based on the codebook's Voronoi partitions. Then, the mutual information between semantic features and their quantized indices is derived in order to maximize semantic information carried by discrete indices. To realize the semantic maximum in practice, an entropy-regularized quantization loss based on empirical estimation is introduced for end-to-end codebook training. Next, the physical channel-induced semantic distortion and the optimal codebook size for semantic communications are characterized under bit-flip errors and semantic distortion. To mitigate the semantic distortion caused by physical channel noise, a novel channel-aware semantic distortion loss is proposed. Simulation results on image reconstruction tasks demonstrate the superior performance of the proposed theoretically-grounded codebook that achieves a 24.1% improvement in peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) and a 46.5% improvement in learned perceptual image patch similarity (LPIPS) compared to the existing codebook designs when the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is 10 dB."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07108","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:02:04Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:02:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baraq","familyName":"Lipshitz","name":"Lipshitz, Baraq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Melone","name":"Melone, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charalampos","familyName":"Maraziaris","name":"Maraziaris, Charalampos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammed","familyName":"Bilal","name":"Bilal, Muhammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Accelerating Sparse Ternary GEMM for Quantized ML on Apple Silicon"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T12:42:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:45:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T15:54:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sparse Ternary General Matrix-Matrix Multiplication (GEMM) remains under-optimized in existing libraries for Apple Silicon CPUs. We present a Sparse Ternary GEMM kernel optimized specifically for Apple's M-series processors. We propose a set of architecture-aware optimizations, including a novel blocked and interleaved sparse data format to improve memory locality, strategies to increase Instruction-Level Parallelism (ILP), and NEON-based Single Instruction Multiple Data (SIMD) vectorization to exploit data-level parallelism. Our scalar implementation achieves up to a 5.98x performance increase over a traditional Ternary Compressed Sparse Column (TCSC) baseline for large matrices with 50% ternary nonzero values (sparsity), reaching up to a 50.2% of the processor's theoretical peak performance, and remains stable across varying sparsity levels. Our vectorized implementation delivers up to a 5.59x performance increase for large matrices with 25% sparsity, and remains stable across varying sparsity levels."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:58:26Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:58:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-15T02:23:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06287","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06287","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06287","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tian","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Tian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum-Theoretical Re-interpretation of Pricing Theory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Pricing of Securities (q-fin.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.3; G.1.10; I.6.5; J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"91G20, 60J35, 47D06, 35R09, 60J75, 60G51, 81S22, 91G80","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:36:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T07:24:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:37:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Motivated by Heisenberg's observable-only stance, we replace latent \"information\" (filtrations, hidden diffusions, state variables) with observable transitions between price states. On a discrete price lattice with a Hilbert-space representation, shift operators and the spectral calculus of the price define observable frequency operators and a translation-invariant convolution generator. Combined with jump operators that encode transition intensities, this yields a completely positive, translation-covariant Lindblad semigroup. Under the risk-neutral condition the framework leads to a nonlocal pricing equation that is diagonal in Fourier space; in the small-mesh diffusive limit its generator converges to the classical Black-Scholes-Merton operator.\n We do not propose another parametric model. We propose a foundation for model construction that is observable, first-principles, and mathematically natural. Noncommutativity emerges from the observable shift algebra rather than being postulated. The jump-intensity ledger determines tail behavior and short-maturity smiles and implies testable links between extreme-event probabilities and implied-volatility wings. Future directions: (i) multi-asset systems on higher-dimensional lattices with vector shifts and block kernels; (ii) state- or flow-dependent kernels as \"financial interactions\" leading to nonlinear master equations while preserving linear risk-neutral pricing; (iii) empirical tests of the predicted scaling relations between jump intensities and market extremes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06287","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:46Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:24:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07044","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07044","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07044","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hendrik Bernd","familyName":"Zarucha","name":"Zarucha, Hendrik Bernd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Caire","name":"Caire, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robustness of Covariance Estimators with Application in Activity Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"68P20, 94A12, 62J","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:11:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T13:25:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:55:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The first part of this work considers a general class of covariance estimators. Each estimator of that class is generated by a real-valued function $g$ and a set of model covariance matrices $H$. If $\\bf{W}$ is a potentially perturbed observation of a searched covariance matrix, then the estimator is the minimizer of the sum of $g$ applied to each eigenvalue of $\\bf{W}^\\frac{1}{2}\\bf{Z}^{-1}\\bf{W}^\\frac{1}{2}$ under the constraint that $\\bf{Z}$ is from $H$. It is shown that under mild conditions on $g$ and $H$ such estimators are robust, meaning the estimation error can be made arbitrarily small if the perturbation of $\\bf{W}$ gets small enough. \\par In the second part of this work the previous results are applied to activity detection in random access with multiple receive antennas. In activity detection recovering the large scale fading coefficients is a sparse recovery problem which can be reduced to a structured covariance estimation problem. The recovery can be done with a non-negative least squares estimator or with a relaxed maximum likelihood estimator. It is shown that under suitable assumptions on the distributions of the noise and the channel coefficients, the relaxed maximum likelihood estimator is from the general class of covariance estimators considered in the first part of this work. Then, codebooks based upon a signed kernel condition are proposed. It is shown that with the proposed codebooks both estimators can recover the large-scale fading coefficients if the number of receive antennas is high enough and $S\\leq\\left\\lceil\\frac{1}{2}M^2\\right\\rceil-1$ where $S$ is the number of active users and $M$ is number of pilot symbols per user."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 1 figure, 4 subfigures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07044","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:31Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:40:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06644","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06644","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06644","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leshu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Leshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayin","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Jiayin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zishen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Zishen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaizhi","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Huaizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingqing","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Pingqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongsen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hongsen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang Katie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yang Katie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RTGS: Real-Time 3D Gaussian Splatting SLAM via Multi-Level Redundancy Reduction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T04:53:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:24:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:47:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:17:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) based Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) systems can largely benefit from 3DGS's state-of-the-art rendering efficiency and accuracy, but have not yet been adopted in resource-constrained edge devices due to insufficient speed. Addressing this, we identify notable redundancies across the SLAM pipeline for acceleration. While conceptually straightforward, practical approaches are required to minimize the overhead associated with identifying and eliminating these redundancies. In response, we propose RTGS, an algorithm-hardware co-design framework that comprehensively reduces the redundancies for real-time 3DGS-SLAM on edge. To minimize the overhead, RTGS fully leverages the characteristics of the 3DGS-SLAM pipeline. On the algorithm side, we introduce (1) an adaptive Gaussian pruning step to remove the redundant Gaussians by reusing gradients computed during backpropagation; and (2) a dynamic downsampling technique that directly reuses the keyframe identification and alpha computing steps to eliminate redundant pixels. On the hardware side, we propose (1) a subtile-level streaming strategy and a pixel-level pairwise scheduling strategy that mitigates workload imbalance via a Workload Scheduling Unit (WSU) guided by previous iteration information; (2) a Rendering and Backpropagation (R\u0026amp;B) Buffer that accelerates the rendering backpropagation by reusing intermediate data computed during rendering; and (3) a Gradient Merging Unit (GMU) to reduce intensive memory accesses caused by atomic operations while enabling pipelined aggregation. Integrated into an edge GPU, RTGS achieves real-time performance (\u0026gt;= 30 FPS) on four datasets and three algorithms, with up to 82.5x energy efficiency over the baseline and negligible quality loss. Code is available at https://github.com/UMN-ZhaoLab/RTGS."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by MICRO2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06644","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:51:06Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:51:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:40:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peize","familyName":"He","name":"He, Peize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichen","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Zichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jiajie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehui","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Zehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuangcheng","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zhuangcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiabing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jiabing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cunxiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Cunxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Conghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AudioMarathon: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Long-Context Audio Understanding and Efficiency in Audio LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T17:50:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T01:03:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Processing long-form audio is a major challenge for Large Audio Language models (LALMs). These models struggle with the quadratic cost of attention ($O(N^2)$) and with modeling long-range temporal dependencies. Existing audio benchmarks are built mostly from short clips and do not evaluate models in realistic long context settings. To address this gap, we introduce AudioMarathon, a benchmark designed to evaluate both understanding and inference efficiency on long-form audio. AudioMarathon provides a diverse set of tasks built upon three pillars: long-context audio inputs with durations ranging from 90.0 to 300.0 seconds, which correspond to encoded sequences of 2,250 to 7,500 audio tokens, respectively, full domain coverage across speech, sound, and music, and complex reasoning that requires multi-hop inference. We evaluate state-of-the-art LALMs and observe clear performance drops as audio length grows. We also study acceleration techniques and analyze the trade-offs of token pruning and KV cache eviction. The results show large gaps across current LALMs and highlight the need for better temporal reasoning and memory-efficient architectures. We believe AudioMarathon will drive the audio and multimodal research community to develop more advanced audio understanding models capable of solving complex audio tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"26 pages, 23 figures, the code is available at \\url{https://github.com/DabDans/AudioMarathon}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:06:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:06:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:06:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07216","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07216","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07216","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Angiuli","name":"Angiuli, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. M.","familyName":"Mangino","name":"Mangino, E. M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Lorenzi","name":"Lorenzi, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"L^p-quasicontractiveness and Kernel estimates for semigroups generated by systems of elliptic operators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Systems of elliptic operators, unbounded coefficients, generation results, consistent strongly continuous analytic semigroups, kernel estimates","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:50:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper focuses on systems of strongly coupled elliptic operators whose coefficients may be unbounded and are defined on a domain $Ω\\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^d$. It is shown that a quasi-contractive semigroup in $L^p$-spaces can be associated with such operators for values of $p$ belonging to an interval that contains $2$ as an interior point. Then, under refined assumptions and considering systems of elliptic operators coupled up to first order, new kernel estimates are established with respect to a distance function that accounts for the growth of the diffusion coefficients and the potential term at infinity."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07216","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:37Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:04:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07205","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07205","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07205","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangshuo","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Fangshuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anastasios","familyName":"Kyrillidis","name":"Kyrillidis, Anastasios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Guided by the Experts: Provable Feature Learning Dynamic of Soft-Routed Mixture-of-Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T16:40:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:59:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures have emerged as a cornerstone of modern AI systems. In particular, MoEs route inputs dynamically to specialized experts whose outputs are aggregated through weighted summation. Despite their widespread application, theoretical understanding of MoE training dynamics remains limited to either separate expert-router optimization or only top-1 routing scenarios with carefully constructed datasets. This paper advances MoE theory by providing convergence guarantees for joint training of soft-routed MoE models with non-linear routers and experts in a student-teacher framework. We prove that, with moderate over-parameterization, the student network undergoes a feature learning phase, where the router's learning process is ``guided'' by the experts, that recovers the teacher's parameters. Moreover, we show that a post-training pruning can effectively eliminate redundant neurons, followed by a provably convergent fine-tuning process that reaches global optimality. To our knowledge, our analysis is the first to bring novel insights in understanding the optimization landscape of the MoE architecture."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07205","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:04:21Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:04:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:04:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanoli","familyName":"Gun","name":"Gun, Sanoli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunil","familyName":"Naik","name":"Naik, Sunil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On divisibility of Hecke eigenvalues of Ikeda lifts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"11F11, 11F30, 11F46, 11F80, 11N56, 11R45","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:24:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this article, we estimate the density of the set of primes $p$ such that the $p$-th Hecke eigenvalue of an Ikeda lift is divisible by a fixed positive integer. One of the main ingredients involves the study of abelian subfields of fixed fields of the kernel of Galois representations attached to elliptic Hecke eigenforms. Further, we study the distribution of Fourier coefficients of elliptic Hecke eigenforms in arithmetic progressions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in Kyushu Journal of Mathematics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:49Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:00:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07055","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07055","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07055","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takao","familyName":"Tomono","name":"Tomono, Takao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Tsujimura","name":"Tsujimura, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Potential of multi-anomalies detection using quantum machine learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:24:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Maintenance of production equipment is critical in manufacturing. Typically, machine learning models are trained on sensor data closely attached to equipment. However, as the number of machines increases, computational cost grows rapidly. In practice, anomalies are often identified by human operators through auditory perception, relying heavily on experience and intuition. In vibration analysis, especially, AR model coefficients combined with one-class SVMs are used for detecting anomalies. In this work, we explore the effect of substituting the classical kernel in the one-class SVM with a quantum kernel. Two experimental setups were used. The first involved a miniature racing car track, where the car passes over a patch of hook-and-loop fastener to generate abnormal sounds, which are recorded using a microphone. The second involved an open-belt drive, where chopsticks are inserted at specific times to produce crushing sounds, simulating sudden anomalies. Our results show a clear advantage of quantum kernels over classical Gaussian (RBF) kernels. On the miniature car track dataset, the quantum kernel achieved an accuracy and F1-score of 0.82, compared to 0.64 and 0.39 respectively for the RBF kernel. For the crushing device, the quantum kernel achieved perfect accuracy and F1-score (1.00), while the RBF kernel reached only 0.64 accuracy and 0.43 F1-score. These findings suggest that quantum kernels enhance the classification accuracy for diverse types of abnormal sound patterns, including both periodic and impulsive anomalies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 6 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07055","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:00:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07052","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07052","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07052","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryan","familyName":"Golbaghi","name":"Golbaghi, Aryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Speech Emotion Recognition via Fine-Tuning Pre-Trained Models and Hyper-Parameter Optimisation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T14:20:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:50:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose a workflow for speech emotion recognition (SER) that combines pre-trained representations with automated hyperparameter optimisation (HPO). Using SpeechBrain wav2vec2-base model fine-tuned on IEMOCAP as the encoder, we compare two HPO strategies, Gaussian Process Bayesian Optimisation (GP-BO) and Tree-structured Parzen Estimators (TPE), under an identical four-dimensional search space and 15-trial budget, with balanced class accuracy (BCA) on the German EmoDB corpus as the objective. All experiments run on 8 CPU cores with 32 GB RAM. GP-BO achieves 0.96 BCA in 11 minutes, and TPE (Hyperopt implementation) attains 0.97 in 15 minutes. In contrast, grid search requires 143 trials and 1,680 minutes to exceed 0.9 BCA, and the best AutoSpeech 2020 baseline reports only 0.85 in 30 minutes on GPU. For cross-lingual generalisation, an EmoDB-trained HPO-tuned model improves zero-shot accuracy by 0.25 on CREMA-D and 0.26 on RAVDESS. Results show that efficient HPO with pre-trained encoders delivers competitive SER on commodity CPUs. Source code to this work is available at: https://github.com/youngaryan/speechbrain-emotion-hpo."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07052","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T02:00:43Z","registered":"2025-10-09T02:00:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T02:00:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07018","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.07018","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.07018","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dung","familyName":"Hoang-Anh","name":"Hoang-Anh, Dung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cuong Pham Trung","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Cuong Pham Trung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianfei","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Jianfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanh-Toan","familyName":"Do","name":"Do, Thanh-Toan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sharpness-Aware Data Generation for Zero-shot Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T13:43:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:48:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Zero-shot quantization aims to learn a quantized model from a pre-trained full-precision model with no access to original real training data. The common idea in zero-shot quantization approaches is to generate synthetic data for quantizing the full-precision model. While it is well-known that deep neural networks with low sharpness have better generalization ability, none of the previous zero-shot quantization works considers the sharpness of the quantized model as a criterion for generating training data. This paper introduces a novel methodology that takes into account quantized model sharpness in synthetic data generation to enhance generalization. Specifically, we first demonstrate that sharpness minimization can be attained by maximizing gradient matching between the reconstruction loss gradients computed on synthetic and real validation data, under certain assumptions. We then circumvent the problem of the gradient matching without real validation set by approximating it with the gradient matching between each generated sample and its neighbors. Experimental evaluations on CIFAR-100 and ImageNet datasets demonstrate the superiority of the proposed method over the state-of-the-art techniques in low-bit quantization settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.07018","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:59:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:59:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:59:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06902","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06902","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06902","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayesha","familyName":"Afzal","name":"Afzal, Ayesha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anna","familyName":"Kahler","name":"Kahler, Anna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georg","familyName":"Hager","name":"Hager, Georg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerhard","familyName":"Wellein","name":"Wellein, Gerhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GROMACS Unplugged: How Power Capping and Frequency Shapes Performance on GPUs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T11:36:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:42:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Molecular dynamics simulations are essential tools in computational biophysics, but their performance depend heavily on hardware choices and configuration. In this work, we presents a comprehensive performance analysis of four NVIDIA GPU accelerators -- A40, A100, L4, and L40 -- using six representative GROMACS biomolecular workloads alongside two synthetic benchmarks: Pi Solver (compute bound) and STREAM Triad (memory bound). We investigate how performance scales with GPU graphics clock frequency and how workloads respond to power capping. The two synthetic benchmarks define the extremes of frequency scaling: Pi Solver shows ideal compute scalability, while STREAM Triad reveals memory bandwidth limits -- framing GROMACS's performance in context. Our results reveal distinct frequency scaling behaviors: Smaller GROMACS systems exhibit strong frequency sensitivity, while larger systems saturate quickly, becoming increasingly memory bound. Under power capping, performance remains stable until architecture- and workload-specific thresholds are reached, with high-end GPUs like the A100 maintaining near-maximum performance even under reduced power budgets. Our findings provide practical guidance for selecting GPU hardware and optimizing GROMACS performance for large-scale MD workflows under power constraints."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06902","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:57:08Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:57:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:57:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felipe","familyName":"Rincón","name":"Rincón, Felipe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Fichtner","name":"Fichtner, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mattia","familyName":"Aleardi","name":"Aleardi, Mattia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Tognarelli","name":"Tognarelli, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eusebio","familyName":"Stucchi","name":"Stucchi, Eusebio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Accelerating wave simulations with neural dispersion correctors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Geophysics (physics.geo-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.6.8; G.1.8; J.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"65M99, 86A15, 35Q68","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:50:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a Fourier neural operator network, designed to correct dispersion errors in numerical wave simulations. The neural dispersion corrector enables the replacement of a computationally expensive high-accuracy simulation by a less expensive low-accuracy simulation. In contrast to neural network surrogates that fully replace a wave equation, the neural dispersion corrector has only a weak dependence on the distribution of model parameters, such as wave speeds. Consequently, the network can be trained with a significantly smaller dataset, while still generalising to unseen input parameters. Following a description of the network architecture and training, we provide examples for the 3-D elastic wave equation. After training with merely 1$\\,$000 examples on one GPU, the neural corrector achieves a speed-up of 16$\\times$ compared to a reference spectral-element simulation and a generalisation to a broad range of strongly heterogeneous wave speed distributions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:38Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06879","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06879","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06879","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natascha","familyName":"Hey","name":"Hey, Natascha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eyal","familyName":"Neuman","name":"Neuman, Eyal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sturmius","familyName":"Tuschmann","name":"Tuschmann, Sturmius","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nonparametric Estimation of Self- and Cross-Impact"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Trading and Market Microstructure (q-fin.TR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Finance (q-fin.MF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Finance (q-fin.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:47:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:40:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce an offline nonparametric estimator for concave multi-asset propagator models based on a dataset of correlated price trajectories and metaorders. Compared to parametric models, our framework avoids parameter explosion in the multi-asset case and yields confidence bounds for the estimator. We implement the estimator using both proprietary metaorder data from Capital Fund Management (CFM) and publicly available S\u0026amp;P order flow data, where we augment the former dataset using a metaorder proxy. In particular, we provide unbiased evidence that self-impact is concave and exhibits a shifted power-law decay, and show that the metaorder proxy stabilizes the calibration. Moreover, we find that introducing cross-impact provides a significant gain in explanatory power, with concave specifications outperforming linear ones, suggesting that the square-root law extends to cross-impact. We also measure asymmetric cross-impact between assets driven by relative liquidity differences. Finally, we demonstrate that a shape-constrained projection of the nonparametric kernel not only ensures interpretability but also slightly outperforms established parametric models in terms of predictive accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 10 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06879","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:35Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santiago Gómez","familyName":"Cobos","name":"Cobos, Santiago Gómez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Ruzhansky","name":"Ruzhansky, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Functional calculus for Safarov pseudo-differential operators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"58J40 (Primary) 35S05, 47A60 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T10:27:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:39:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given a smooth, closed Riemannian manifold $(M,g)$ equipped with a linear connection $\\nabla$ (not necessarily metric), we develop the holomorphic functional calculus for operators belonging to the global pseudo-differential classes $Ψ_{ρ, δ}^m\\left(Ω^κ, \\nabla, τ\\right)$ introduced by Safarov. As a consequence of our main result, we establish a Szegö type-theorem, derive asymptotic expansion of the heat kernel trace, and calculate some associated spectral $ζ$-functions."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:56:08Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:56:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:56:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06834","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06834","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06834","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vasileios","familyName":"Titopoulos","name":"Titopoulos, Vasileios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kosmas","familyName":"Alexandridis","name":"Alexandridis, Kosmas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgos","familyName":"Dimitrakopoulos","name":"Dimitrakopoulos, Giorgos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vectorized FlashAttention with Low-cost Exponential Computation in RISC-V Vector Processors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:55:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:37:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Attention is a core operation in numerous machine learning and artificial intelligence models. This work focuses on the acceleration of attention kernel using FlashAttention algorithm, in vector processors, particularly those based on the RISC-V instruction set architecture (ISA). This work represents the first effort to vectorize FlashAttention, minimizing scalar code and simplifying the computational complexity of evaluating exponentials needed by softmax used in attention. By utilizing a low-cost approximation for exponentials in floating-point arithmetic, we reduce the cost of computing the exponential function without the need to extend baseline vector ISA with new custom instructions. Also, appropriate tiling strategies are explored with the goal to improve memory locality. Experimental results highlight the scalability of our approach, demonstrating significant performance gains with the vectorized implementations when processing attention layers in practical applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06834","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:55:32Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:55:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:55:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06803","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06803","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06803","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliška","familyName":"Krátká","name":"Krátká, Eliška","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aurél Gábor","familyName":"Gábris","name":"Gábris, Aurél Gábor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum Computing Methods for Malware Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:31:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-031-83157-7_8","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we explore the potential of quantum computing in enhancing malware detection through the application of Quantum Machine Learning (QML). Our main objective is to investigate the performance of the Quantum Support Vector Machine (QSVM) algorithm compared to SVM. A publicly available dataset containing raw binaries of Portable Executable (PE) files was used for the classification. The QSVM algorithm, incorporating quantum kernels through different feature maps, was implemented and evaluated on a local simulator within the Qiskit SDK and IBM quantum computers. Experimental results from simulators and quantum hardware provide insights into the behavior and performance of quantum computers, especially in handling large-scale computations for malware detection tasks. The work summarizes the practical experience with using quantum hardware via the Qiskit interfaces. We describe in detail the critical issues encountered, as well as the fixes that had to be developed and applied to the base code of the Qiskit Machine Learning library. These issues include missing transpilation of the circuits submitted to IBM Quantum systems and exceeding the maximum job size limit due to the submission of all the circuits in one job."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 2 figures, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06803","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:54:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06802","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06802","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06802","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Islomjon","familyName":"Shukhratov","name":"Shukhratov, Islomjon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Gorinsky","name":"Gorinsky, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Capture and Interact: Rapid 3D Object Acquisition and Rendering with Gaussian Splatting in Unity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T09:31:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:36:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Capturing and rendering three-dimensional (3D) objects in real time remain a significant challenge, yet hold substantial potential for applications in augmented reality, digital twin systems, remote collaboration and prototyping. We present an end-to-end pipeline that leverages 3D Gaussian Splatting (3D GS) to enable rapid acquisition and interactive rendering of real-world objects using a mobile device, cloud processing and a local computer. Users scan an object with a smartphone video, upload it for automated 3D reconstruction, and visualize it interactively in Unity at an average of 150 frames per second (fps) on a laptop. The system integrates mobile capture, cloud-based 3D GS and Unity rendering to support real-time telepresence. Our experiments show that the pipeline processes scans in approximately 10 minutes on a graphics processing unit (GPU) achieving real-time rendering on the laptop."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06802","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:54:47Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:54:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:54:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06767","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06767","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06767","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["International Institute of Information Technology Bangalore"],"givenName":"Bindu G","familyName":"Gowda","name":"Gowda, Bindu G","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["International Institute of Information Technology Bangalore"],"givenName":"Yogesh","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Yogesh","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["International Institute of Information Technology Bangalore"],"givenName":"Yash","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Yash","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["International Institute of Information Technology Bangalore"],"givenName":"Madhav","familyName":"Rao","name":"Rao, Madhav","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hardware-Efficient CNNs: Interleaved Approximate FP32 Multipliers for Kernel Computation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T08:49:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:34:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Single-precision floating point (FP32) data format, defined by the IEEE 754 standard, is widely employed in scientific computing, signal processing, and deep learning training, where precision is critical. However, FP32 multiplication is computationally expensive and requires complex hardware, especially for precisely handling mantissa multiplication. In practical applications like neural network inference, perfect accuracy is not always necessary, minor multiplication errors often have little impact on final accuracy. This enables trading precision for gains in area, power, and speed. This work focuses on CNN inference using approximate FP32 multipliers, where the mantissa multiplication is approximated by employing error-variant approximate compressors, that significantly reduce hardware cost. Furthermore, this work optimizes CNN performance by employing differently approximated FP32 multipliers and studying their impact when interleaved within the kernels across the convolutional layers. The placement and ordering of these approximate multipliers within each kernel are carefully optimized using the Non-dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm-II, balancing the trade-off between accuracy and hardware efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06767","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:53:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06734","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06734","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06734","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian","familyName":"Göttsch","name":"Göttsch, Fabian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Franke","name":"Franke, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Pourdamghani","name":"Pourdamghani, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Caire","name":"Caire, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimizing Fronthaul Quantization for Flexible User Load in Cell-Free Massive MIMO"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T07:46:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:31:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the physical layer (PHY) spectral efficiency and fronthaul network load of a scalable user-centric cell-free massive MIMO system. Each user-centric cluster processor responsible for cluster-level signal processing is located at one of multiple decentralized units (DUs). Thus, the radio units in the cluster must exchange data with the corresponding DU over the fronthaul. Because the fronthaul links have limited capacity, this data must be quantized before it is sent over the fronthaul. We consider a routed fronthaul network, where the cluster processor placement and fronthaul traffic routing are jointly optimized with a mixed-integer linear program. For different numbers of users in the network, we investigate the effect of fronthaul quantization rates, a system parameter computed based on rate-distortion theory. Our results show that with optimized quantization rates, the fronthaul load is quite stable for a wide range of user loads without significant PHY performance loss. This demonstrates that the cell-free massive MIMO PHY and fronthaul network are resilient to varying user densities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06734","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:53:12Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:53:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:53:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06590","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06590","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06590","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Ziyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"DanDan","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, DanDan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixiang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Kaixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weilong","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Weilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianxin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jianxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Libin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Libin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongjie","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Yongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taozhi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Taozhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiajia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingpei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qingpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingdong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Ming-UniVision: Joint Image Understanding and Generation with a Unified Continuous Tokenizer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T02:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:19:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual tokenization remains a core challenge in unifying visual understanding and generation within the autoregressive paradigm. Existing methods typically employ tokenizers in discrete latent spaces to align with the tokens from large language models, where the quantization errors can limit semantic expressiveness and degrade the capability of vision-language understanding. To address this, we introduce MingTok, a new family of visual tokenizers with a continuous latent space, for unified autoregressive generation and understanding. While understanding tasks favor discriminative high-dimensional features, generation tasks prefer compact low-level codes. Thus, to reconcile these competing demands, MingTok adopts a three-stage sequential architecture involving low-level encoding, semantic expansion, and visual reconstruction. Built on top of it, Ming-UniVision eliminates the need for task-specific visual representations, and unifies diverse vision-language tasks under a single autoregrsssive prediction paradigm. By formulating both understanding and generation as next-token prediction in a shared continuous space, it seamlessly supports multi-round, in-context tasks such as iterative understanding, generation and editing. Empirically, we find that using a unified continuous visual representation reconciles the competing requirements on the tokenizers by the understanding and generation tasks, thereby leading to state-of-the-art level performance across both domains. We hope our findings will facilitate unified visual tokenization in the continuous domain. Inference code and model weights are released to benefit community."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code released at https://github.com/inclusionAI/Ming-UniVision"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06590","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:48Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:49:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06558","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06558","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06558","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacong","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Jiacong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eloy de Lera","familyName":"Acedo","name":"Acedo, Eloy de Lera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaan","familyName":"Artuc","name":"Artuc, Kaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuelei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xuelei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RFSoC receiver calibration system for 21-cm global spectrum experiments from space: The CosmoCube case"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T01:22:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:16:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1093/rasti/rzae064","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The CosmoCube project plans to deploy a global 21-cm spectrometer with 10-100 MHz observation band in a lunar orbit. The farside part of such an orbit, i.e. the part of orbit behind the Moon, offers an ideal site for accurately measuring the 21-cm signal from the Dark Ages, Cosmic Dawn and Epoch of Reionization, as the effects of the Earth's ionosphere, artificial radio frequency interference (RFI), and complex terrain and soil are all avoided. Given the limitations of a satellite platform, we propose a receiver calibration system design based on a Radio Frequency system-on-chip, consisting of a Vector Network Analyzer (VNA) sub-system, and a source switching sub-system. We introduce the measurement principle of the VNA, and discuss the effect of quantization error. The accuracy, stability and trajectory noise of the VNA are tested in laboratory experiments. We also present the design of the source-switching sub-system, generating mock datasets, showing that the imperfect return loss, insertion loss, and isolation of surface-mounted microwave switches have a minimal effect on the sky foreground fitting residuals, which are within $\\pm10$ mK under optimal fitting condition. When all possible measurement errors in reflection coefficients and physical temperatures are taken into account, the foreground fitting residuals for the 50-90 MHz part of the spectrum remain around $\\pm20$ mK."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 20 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06558","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:49:04Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:49:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:49:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06532","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06532","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06532","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqi","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Hanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huaqin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huaqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingfeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yingfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CLAQS: Compact Learnable All-Quantum Token Mixer with Shared-ansatz for Text Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:14:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum compute is scaling fast, from cloud QPUs to high throughput GPU simulators, making it timely to prototype quantum NLP beyond toy tasks. However, devices remain qubit limited and depth limited, training can be unstable, and classical attention is compute and memory heavy. This motivates compact, phase aware quantum token mixers that stabilize amplitudes and scale to long sequences. We present CLAQS, a compact, fully quantum token mixer for text classification that jointly learns complex-valued mixing and nonlinear transformations within a unified quantum circuit. To enable stable end-to-end optimization, we apply l1 normalization to regulate amplitude scaling and introduce a two-stage parameterized quantum architecture that decouples shared token embeddings from a window-level quantum feed-forward module. Operating under a sliding-window regime with document-level aggregation, CLAQS requires only eight data qubits and shallow circuits, yet achieves 91.64% accuracy on SST-2 and 87.08% on IMDB, outperforming both classical Transformer baselines and strong hybrid quantum-classical counterparts."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06532","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:48:28Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:48:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:48:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06429","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06429","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06429","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linlin","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Linlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeping","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Zeping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michail","familyName":"Matthaiou","name":"Matthaiou, Michail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Detection and Bandwidth Allocation with Hybrid Quantized and Full-Precision Observations over Multiplicative Fading Channels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:09:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:07:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A hybrid detector that fuses both quantized and full-precision observations is proposed for weak signal detection under additive and multiplicative Gaussian noise. We first derive a locally most powerful test (LMPT)--based hybrid detector from the composite probability distribution of the compound observations received by the fusion center, and then analyze its asymptotic detection performance. Subsequently, we optimize the sensor-wise quantization thresholds to achieve near-optimal asymptotic performance at the local sensor level. Moreover, we propose a mixed-integer linear programming approach to solve the optimization problem of transmission bandwidth allocation accounting for bandwidth constraints and error-prone channels. Finally, simulation results demonstrate the superiority of the proposed hybrid detector and the bandwidth allocation strategy, especially in challenging error-prone channel conditions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 4 figures, submitted to IEEE TVT"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06429","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:46:05Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:46:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:46:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06421","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06421","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06421","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Abdullah","familyName":"Soomro","name":"Soomro, Muhammad Abdullah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fatima Muhammad","familyName":"Anwar","name":"Anwar, Fatima Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Breaking Precision Time: OS Vulnerability Exploits Against IEEE 1588"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T20:00:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:06:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Precision Time Protocol (PTP), standardized as IEEE 1588, provides sub-microsecond synchronization across distributed systems and underpins critical infrastructure in telecommunications, finance, power systems, and industrial automation. While prior work has extensively analyzed PTP's vulnerability to network-based attacks, prompting the development of cryptographic protections and anomaly detectors, these defenses presume an uncompromised host. In this paper, we identify and exploit a critical blind spot in current threat models: kernel-level adversaries operating from within the host running the PTP stack. We present the first systematic study of kernel-rooted attacks on PTP, demonstrating how privileged attackers can manipulate system time by corrupting key interfaces without altering PTP network traffic. We implement three attack primitives, constant offset, progressive skew, and random jitter, using in-kernel payloads, and evaluate their impact on the widely used ptp4l and phc2sys daemons. Our experiments reveal that these attacks can silently destabilize clock synchronization, bypassing existing PTP security extensions. These findings highlight the urgent need to reconsider host-level trust assumptions and integrate kernel integrity into the design of secure time synchronization systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in IEEE ISPCS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06421","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:53Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:45:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06405","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06405","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06405","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Loi","name":"Loi, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roberto","familyName":"Mossa","name":"Mossa, Roberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"ZUddas","name":"ZUddas, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Bergman metric of Cartan-Hartogs domains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Complex Variables (math.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T19:34:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:06:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the Bergman metric and introduce the Bergman dual on Cartan-Hartogs (CH) domains. For a bounded domain D in C^n with Bergman kernel K_D, we define the Bergman dual of (D, g_D) as (D*, g_D*), where D* is the maximal domain on which the modified kernel K_D*(z, zbar) = K_D(z, -zbar) is positive, and g_D* is the Kahler metric obtained from K_D*. For a Cartan-Hartogs domain M_{Omega, mu} we prove the equivalence of: (i) M_{Omega, mu} is biholomorphic to the unit ball; (ii) its Bergman metric is a Kahler-Ricci soliton; (iii) after rescaling by a constant factor, the Bergman dual is finitely projectively induced. Conditions (i) and (ii) are Bergman-metric analogues of classical rigidity for Kahler-Einstein metrics (related to Yau's problem and Cheng's conjecture) and to recent rigidity for Kahler-Ricci solitons. Condition (iii) emphasizes the duality viewpoint, inspired by bounded symmetric domains and their compact duals. We also compare our results with other canonical metrics on CH domains, namely g_{Omega, mu} and hat g_{Omega, mu}, and discuss open problems about the maximal domain on which the Bergman dual is defined."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The paper consists of 19 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06405","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:45:32Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:45:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:45:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young D.","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Young D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Mehrotra","name":"Mehrotra, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malcolm","familyName":"Chadwick","name":"Chadwick, Malcolm","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alberto Gil","familyName":"Ramos","name":"Ramos, Alberto Gil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sourav","familyName":"Bhattacharya","name":"Bhattacharya, Sourav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient High-Resolution Image Editing with Hallucination-Aware Loss and Adaptive Tiling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:09:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-resolution (4K) image-to-image synthesis has become increasingly important for mobile applications. Existing diffusion models for image editing face significant challenges, in terms of memory and image quality, when deployed on resource-constrained devices. In this paper, we present MobilePicasso, a novel system that enables efficient image editing at high resolutions, while minimising computational cost and memory usage. MobilePicasso comprises three stages: (i) performing image editing at a standard resolution with hallucination-aware loss, (ii) applying latent projection to overcome going to the pixel space, and (iii) upscaling the edited image latent to a higher resolution with adaptive context-preserving tiling. Our user study with 46 participants reveals that MobilePicasso not only improves image quality by 18-48% but reduces hallucinations by 14-51% over existing methods. MobilePicasso demonstrates significantly lower latency, e.g., up to 55.8$\\times$ speed-up, yet with a small increase in runtime memory, e.g., a mere 9% increase over prior work. Surprisingly, the on-device runtime of MobilePicasso is observed to be faster than a server-based high-resolution image editing model running on an A100 GPU."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:57Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06275","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06275","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06275","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjan","familyName":"Mishra","name":"Mishra, Ranjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julian I.","familyName":"Bibo","name":"Bibo, Julian I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quinten","familyName":"van Engelen","name":"van Engelen, Quinten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henk","familyName":"Schaapman","name":"Schaapman, Henk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reproducibility Study of \"XRec: Large Language Models for Explainable Recommendation\""}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:07:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this study, we reproduced the work done in the paper \"XRec: Large Language Models for Explainable Recommendation\" by Ma et al. (2024). The original authors introduced XRec, a model-agnostic collaborative instruction-tuning framework that enables large language models (LLMs) to provide users with comprehensive explanations of generated recommendations. Our objective was to replicate the results of the original paper, albeit using Llama 3 as the LLM for evaluation instead of GPT-3.5-turbo. We built on the source code provided by Ma et al. (2024) to achieve our goal. Our work extends the original paper by modifying the input embeddings or deleting the output embeddings of XRec's Mixture of Experts module. Based on our results, XRec effectively generates personalized explanations and its stability is improved by incorporating collaborative information. However, XRec did not consistently outperform all baseline models in every metric. Our extended analysis further highlights the importance of the Mixture of Experts embeddings in shaping the explanation structures, showcasing how collaborative signals interact with language modeling. Through our work, we provide an open-source evaluation implementation that enhances accessibility for researchers and practitioners alike. Our complete code repository can be found at https://github.com/julianbibo/xrec-reproducibility."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06275","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:29Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06268","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06268","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06268","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Rapagnani","name":"Rapagnani, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chemseddine","familyName":"Ananna","name":"Ananna, Chemseddine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonino","familyName":"Di Leva","name":"Di Leva, Antonino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianluca","familyName":"Imbriani","name":"Imbriani, Gianluca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthias","familyName":"Junker","name":"Junker, Matthias","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Pignatari","name":"Pignatari, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Best","name":"Best, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SHADES -- $^{22}$Ne($α$,n)$^{25}$Mg reaction rate in the Gamow window"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:28:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:01:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1051/epjconf/202226011031","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neutron capture reactions are the main contributors to the synthesis of the heavy elements through the s-process. Together with $^{13}$C($α$,n)16O, which has recently been measured by the LUNA collaboration in an energy region inside the Gamow peak, 22Ne(α,n)25Mg is the other main neutron source in stars. Its cross section is mostly unknown in the relevant stellar energy (450 keV \u0026lt; Ecm \u0026lt; 750 keV), where only upper limits from direct experiments and highly uncertain estimates from indirect sources exist. The ERC project SHADES (UniNa/INFN) aims to provide for the first time direct cross section data in this region and to reduce the uncertainties of higher energy resonance parameters. High sensitivity measurements will be performed with the new LUNA-MV accelerator at the INFN-LNGS laboratory in Italy: the energy sensitivity of the SHADES hybrid neutron detector, together with the low background environment of the LNGS and the high beam current of the new accelerator promises to improve the sensitivity by over 2 orders of magnitude over the state of the art, allowing to finally probe the unexplored low-energy cross section. Here we present an overview of the project and first results on the setup characterization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures, NIC-VI conference proceeding"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06268","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:19Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06256","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06256","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06256","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas R.","familyName":"Allgood","name":"Allgood, Nicholas R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Commuting Hamiltonian Framework for Quantum Time Transfer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Operator Algebras (math.OA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:53:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a mathematical framework for quantum time transfer based on commuting families of Hamiltonians and synchronization observables. The synchronization subspace is defined as the kernel of a difference operator between local clocks, and we show that this subspace is preserved exactly by a commutative $*$-subalgebra of Hamiltonians compatible with the clocks. Our first main result establishes \\emph{perturbative stability}: for $ε$-compatible dynamics, where the commutator with the synchronization operator is bounded in norm by $ε$, we prove quantitative drift bounds showing that timing correlations degrade at most linearly in time with slope proportional to $ε$. Our second main result provides a \\emph{representation-theoretic classification}: in the presence of a finite group symmetry, the synchronization subspace coincides with the diagonal isotypic component in the tensor product decomposition, and synchronization preservation is characterized by the commutant algebra of the group action. These results identify synchronization as a structural invariant of operator algebras, connecting approximate commutation, kernel-preserving dynamics, and symmetry protection. Beyond quantum time transfer, the framework suggests categorical and resource-theoretic generalizations and contributes to the broader study of operator-algebraic invariants in multipartite quantum dynamics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06256","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-09T01:42:03Z","registered":"2025-10-09T01:42:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-09T01:42:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":57,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-08T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-09T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28GPU+OR+GPUs+OR+accelerator+OR+accelerators+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+%22distributed+training%22+OR+%22model+serving%22+OR+%22KV+cache%22+OR+%22KV-cache%22+OR+quantization+OR+%22speculative+decoding%22+OR+%22tensor+parallel%22+OR+%22pipeline+parallel%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}